<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Drina is a river of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, about 346 km long, formed by the Tara and Piva and flowing north into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>. For much of its course it marks the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. The Ottoman bridge at Višegrad, built for Mehmed Pasha Sokolović in 1577, inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivo-andric">Ivo Andrić</a>'s novel The Bridge on the Drina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drina.jpg" alt="Drina" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earlywho · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Кањон_Дрине_-_Гладина.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Drina is the longest tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> and the longest karst river of the Dinaric Alps, and it belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> drainage basin. It rises where the Tara and the Piva meet, between Šćepan Polje in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and Hum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. Measured from the source of the Tara, its longer headwater, it is 487 kilometres long. It is a fast, cold river with green water, running through limestone gorges, among them the Međeđa gorge, where it is only 12 metres wide at Tijesno. Near its mouth it spreads across the largest flood plain in former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and it once entered the Sava near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabac">Šabac</a>, 30 kilometres east of the present mouth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name comes from the Roman name Drinus, which derives from Greek and ultimately from a native name of Illyrian origin. Boats were long the main traffic on the river. In the second half of the 17th century the Ottoman traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a> wrote that people in the valley hollowed oak trunks into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugout-canoe">dugout canoes</a>, with thousands of them at Zvornik sailing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. In the 19th century loggers floated rafts of trunks down the Tara and the Drina to the sawmill at Foča.</p><p>Excavations from 2012 to 2015 at Orlovine near Mali Zvornik showed that stone ramparts above the river belonged to a large Byzantine city dating from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat with one of the largest Byzantine churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. In September 1914 the river was the scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cer">Battle of Cer</a> and the Battle of Drina between Serbian and Austro-Hungarian armies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river forms much of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Its main towns include Foča, Goražde, Višegrad, Zvornik, Bajina Bašta and Loznica, and three hydroelectric stations dam it. The Mehmed Paša Sokolović Bridge at Višegrad, designed by the court architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a>, has been on the UNESCO World Heritage List since 2007. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivo-andric">Ivo Andrić</a>'s 1945 novel Na Drini ćuprija (The Bridge on the Drina) is set around it, and Stanislav Binički's march honouring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cer">Battle of Cer</a> is known as the March on the Drina.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Drina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Slobodan Milošević had wished to make it Serbia's anthem, but the Socialist Party of Serbia chose &quot;March on the <b>Drina</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hey-slavs">Hey, Slavs</a></li><li><blockquote>The Autariatae lived in the upper <b>Drina</b> and Lim valleys, in modern western Serbia, Bosnia and Montenegro, by the 5th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/autariatae">Autariatae</a></li></ul></section>
